Below is a minimal, self‑contained JavaScript snippet that shows how to create a **data accessor** for your `BarChart` component and then use that accessor to read and write the chart’s `DataPoints` and `Labels`.

```js
/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Create the accessor
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */
const barChartAccessor = chartDataAccessorFactory({
  chart:      'BarChart',          // the component name
  library:    'visualizationLibrary', // the library instance (as a string)
  configObject: 'config'           // the configuration object name
});

/* -------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Use the accessor
   ------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* 2a. Read current values */
const currentDataPoints = barChartAccessor.getDataPoints(); // <-- returns the array of data points
const currentLabels     = barChartAccessor.getLabels();     // <-- returns the array of labels

console.log('Current DataPoints:', currentDataPoints);
console.log('Current Labels:', currentLabels);

/* 2b. Update values */
const newDataPoints = [10, 20, 30, 40];
const newLabels     = ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4'];

barChartAccessor.setDataPoints(newDataPoints); // <-- updates the chart’s data points
barChartAccessor.setLabels(newLabels);         // <-- updates the chart’s labels

/* 2c. Verify the update */
console.log('Updated DataPoints:', barChartAccessor.getDataPoints());
console.log('Updated Labels:', barChartAccessor.getLabels());
```

### What this does

| Step | Action | Result |
|------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Calls `chartDataAccessorFactory` with the chart name, library instance, and config object name. | Returns an accessor object that knows how to talk to the `BarChart` component. |
| 2a | Calls `getDataPoints()` and `getLabels()` on the accessor. | Retrieves the current data points and labels from the chart. |
| 2b | Calls `setDataPoints()` and `setLabels()` on the accessor. | Pushes new values into the chart via the configuration object. |
| 2c | Reads back the values to confirm the update. | Shows that the chart’s state has changed. |

> **Tip:**  
> If your library exposes the chart instance directly (e.g., `visualizationLibrary.BarChart`), you can replace the string `'visualizationLibrary'` with the actual object reference. The factory will still work because it only needs a reference to the library to locate the component.

Feel free to adapt the property names (`getDataPoints`, `setDataPoints`, etc.) if your accessor uses a different API. The key idea is that the factory gives you a convenient wrapper around the chart’s configuration object.
